  "query_preview": "..\nKey Message 1: Severe weather threat remains high for Tuesday\nmorning through Tuesday night.\n\nOn satellite and radar imagery this afternoon, very strong MCSs \nwere ongoing over the Great Lakes region into the OH Valley, and\ndebris clouds were already moving through our forecast area. \nStorms in Chicagoland are extremely severe with 100 mph listed \nin the warnings, also possibly tornadic. Strong forcing for \nascent was partially coming from an area of strong positive \nvorticity advection aloft along with a robust mid/upper level \njet. High resolution guidance continues to indicate that a \nvariation of this activity will dive SSE tonight and into the \novernight hours, reaching SE WV and SW VA around 2 to 5 AM.\n\nCloser to our neck of the woods and in the lower levels, the  \nprimary area of low pressure will be located over the Delmarva \nPeninsula tomorrow, while a lee trough will extend from the low\nSSW along the Piedmont. This trough will be out ahead of a cold\nfront moving through the TN Valley and central and southern \nAppalachians. These features will support widespread heavy \nrain and strong to severe thunderstorms, which may contain \nsevere with damaging winds, in the form of one of more bowing \nlinear MCSs traveling from the OH Valley. CAMs disagree on the \nevolution of these features, and it is possible the worst of it \nstays mostly to our west. However, areas especially west of a \nline from Lewisburg, WV to Martinsville will have the highest \nchance for severe weather in the morning hours. MUCAPE right \nahead of the bowing segments of the MCS(s) look to be over 4000 \nJ/kg, and the \n\nAs the day progresses, the cold front moves south towards the  \nregion, along with a northward lifting warm front from the \nwest. Strong short waves will continue to fuel redevelopment in \nthe afternoon and evening. Severe weather in the afternoon will \ndepend on how long the morning convection lasts and how much \ntime the atmosphere has to recover ahead of the front/next upper\nlevel wave. It appears we should have at least a few hours with\nbreaks in cloud cover before the front arrives in the \nafternoon. \n\nThe ECMWF EFI and Shift of Tails for CAPE/shear continues to  \nbullseye almost the entire forecast area with abnormally strong \nCAPE and shear. Deep layer shear will be around 40 to 50 kts, \nwhich will support supercells with damaging winds and large \nhail. The current timing of the boundaries and diving short wave\nenergy/jet streaks are also around peak heating. Finally, there\nwill be a tornado threat generally east of a Lynchburg to \nDanville line...closer to where the warm front will be in the \nafternoon. We continue to be outlooked on Tuesday for a Slight \nRisk of severe weather from SPC, and a Marginal Risk of\nexcessive rainfall from WPC for flash flooding.\n\nThe front and trough axis moves quickly southeast and out of the \nforecast area by midnight Tuesday. Upslope flow may bring a few \nshowers to the mountains overnight, but the severe threat will \nbe over. Thursday and Friday also look quiet, with a return to \nscattered afternoon showers and storms for the weekend.",
